omen’s bodies are fair game for commentary, and that’s a message that hurts both the boys (who miss a lesson in empathy) and anyone who stands out physically in a team setting. The goal isn’t to punish, but to redirect. So, you might say to the coach exactly what you said to me: *“Look, I know it’s normal for people to notice someone’s mum, but the way it’s being talked about on the team feels off to me—it’s repetitive and might make her uncomfortable. Can you just remind the lads about respectful speech?”* That’s not overreach; it’s community care. Does that distinction make sense? I’m not trying to blow it out of proportion—just to hold two truths at once: noticing attractiveness is normal, and repeated, public commentary about someone who’s just there as a parent can be both disrespectful and instructive in a way we don’t want for kids.
