23:32:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you—and there’s a grain of truth in what you’re saying. Hormones do amplify curiosity and attraction in teenagers, and noticing attractiveness isn't something anyone needs to be ashamed of or "unteach." That’s absolutely natural. But there’s a big difference between a private, internal reaction and a repeated, public, and audible commentary about a specific adult in a shared community space. One is a passing thought; the other is a behaviour that affects real people and sets a social norm. Nature gives us impulses; it doesn’t dictate manners, boundaries, or respect. Those are learned, and they’re part of what we teach young people as they grow into adulthood. When the boys’ talk becomes something other parents and kids overhear, it stops being just private “wiring” and becomes a social act with consequences. The mum in question might feel self-conscious or unwelcome. The boys, meanwhile, are practising a habit of openly objectifying a woman without any awareness of how that impacts her or those around her. That habit, left unaddressed, can harden into an assumption that it’s okay to comment on women’s bodies in any setting—work, school, sport—because “it’s just nature.” The coach doesn’t need to shame them or pretend hormones don’t exist. A brief, neutral reminder like “We speak respectfully about everyone here—that includes parents” doesn’t crush their natural development; it channels it into acceptable boundaries. It’s the same way we teach kids that you don’t say everything you think out loud about someone’s weight, disability, or fashion choices, even if you notice those things naturally. Attraction isn’t an exception. So I’m not suggesting the coach “unteach” biology. I’m suggesting he reinforce a simple, lifelong skill: treating people as more than their appearance, especially in a shared setting where everyone deserves to feel comfortable. That’s not suppressing nature—it’s building character alongside it.
